+
+## Snapshot Storage
+
+The active database file lives at a stable kiosk-local path configured at install time (e.g., `/var/lib/gaugdesk/registry.db`). Temporary databases during import use a distinct path pattern (e.g., `/var/lib/gaugedesk/registry.tmp.XXXXXX`). After validation succeeds, the temporary database replaces the active one in a single filesystem operation. If no database exists (fresh install), the first valid import activates it.
+
+## Record Schema
+
+Each `records` table row contains the fields from the snapshot JSON, plus an internal row ID. A primary key on `serial` (the normalized form stored at import time) enables efficient exact-match lookups. All other snapshot fields are stored as-is.
+
+## Error Handling
+
+Import failures (invalid signature, unsupported schema, duplicate serial) abort without touching the active database. The error message identifies the specific failure type and includes the line number of the first duplicate or malformed record when applicable. Lookup errors (database corrupted, disk I/O failure) are surfaced to the caller as a lookup exception; the behavior of the caller determines whether the lookup is retried.
+
+## Performance & Capacity
+
+The 250 ms lookup performance goal is achieved through SQLite's B-tree index on the `serial` column, which provides O(log n) lookups on 90,000 records. The entire active database (150 MB snapshot + index overhead) fits in memory on a 4 GB kiosk, so subsequent lookups after the first cache hit remain subsecond. Snapshot imports are I/O bound; validation and indexing are background operations that do not block the UI.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Snapshot index backend | Local embedded SQLite | On-site HTTPS directory, Managed search service | Local SQLite provides predictable O(log n) lookups and no network dependency, meeting the 250 ms goal and offline-only tenet. Each kiosk validates and builds its index independently, keeping snapshot updates self-contained within the registry component. No service to operate, no credentials, no recurring cost. The trade-off is that each kiosk must have disk space for the database and verify the snapshot independently; this is acceptable since snapshot validation is necessary anyway. |
